  "summary": "Gothic Revival style incorporating Scottish castle elements.",
  "description": "Characterized by its dramatic turrets, battlements, and asymmetrical facades with Scottish Renaissance details. Developed as a romantic interpretation of Scottish castle architecture by British aristocracy seeking connection with Scotland's cultural heritage.",
  "culturalContext": "Scottish Baronial (16th-18th c., revived 19th) mixed medieval tower-house defense with Renaissance turrets: crow-stepped gables, corbelled round turrets at corners (bartizans), conical caps and massive walling — Craigievar, Castle Fraser, Glamis — then revived by Edinburgh's New Town fringes and Balmoral for Victorian royal romanticism. The style's \"fairytale fortress\" profile became Scotland's exported image (highland hotels, Whisky-box baronial), engineered comfort beneath martial costume.",
